                                                                              153/185
  Why the chained stub rows red ONE row per extra access: each stub row compares the flat answer before its lca access
  with the one after; an extra access shifts the parity from that point on, and the NEXT row compares two flats that
  bracket one access again.  Why `tailr' (4f, one reply queued) stays green under every read duplicate: the second read
  finds the queue empty and returns last_scancode -- the byte just popped (the Opus seat, measured).  Why the heads stay
  green: they land on 0x5f (unmapped) or 0x3ff SCR (inert) -- the EQUIVALENT cells.

A red row outside its prediction is re-run ALONE before it is believed.
